<TopicPage slug="subjects/biology/foundational/classification-tree-of-life">

  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is a beginner lesson in Biology. It needs zero prior knowledge. You
    already know the raw material: a pet, a houseplant, the mould on an old
    orange, the weeds in a footpath. This lesson is about putting names on all
    of that — and discovering that the naming system hides a family tree.
  </p>
  <p>By the end of this lesson you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>list the seven things every living thing does, using the memory aid MRS GREN;</li>
    <li>say what classification is for, and write a two-part scientific name correctly;</li>
    <li>follow a dichotomous key to name an unknown organism;</li>
    <li>sort an organism into one of the five major groups by its visible features;</li>
    <li>read a branching tree diagram and say what its branch points mean.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What makes something alive?</h2>
  <p>
    Start with a trick question. A candle flame moves, and it consumes fuel. A
    salt crystal grows. A river takes in water all along its length. Yet none
    of them is alive. So what separates a candle from a cat?
  </p>
  <p>
    Biologists answer with a checklist, not a single test. A living thing — an
    <strong>organism</strong> — does <em>all</em> of seven things. Memory
    aid: <strong>MRS GREN</strong>.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Movement</strong> — moving itself, or moving substances inside itself (a plant pumps water, even rooted in place).</li>
    <li><strong>Respiration</strong> — releasing energy from food by chemical reactions. This is not "breathing"; breathing is just one way of bringing oxygen in.</li>
    <li><strong>Sensitivity</strong> — noticing and responding to changes around it (a <em>stimulus</em> is such a change), from a sunflower tracking light to a bacterium swimming towards sugar.</li>
    <li><strong>Growth</strong> — a permanent increase in size by building new material, not just swelling up.</li>
    <li><strong>Reproduction</strong> — producing new individuals, with a partner or alone.</li>
    <li><strong>Excretion</strong> — getting rid of the waste its own chemistry creates, such as carbon dioxide (CO<sub>2</sub>) and urea.</li>
    <li><strong>Nutrition</strong> — taking in or making its food: animals eat, fungi absorb, plants make sugar from sunlight.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Now test the checklist on something puzzling. A <strong>virus</strong> —
    say, the flu virus — fails most of the list. It does not respire, excrete
    or grow. It is a packaged set of instructions that hijacks another cell's
    machinery. That is why biologists class viruses as non-living, and why
    "is a virus alive?" is a favourite exam question: it tests whether you
    really understand the checklist.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> a candle moves and a crystal grows, but only an
    organism does all seven MRS GREN jobs. Now: once something is alive, how
    do we file it?
  </p>

  <h2>The filing system for life</h2>
  <p>
    A drop of pond water or a patch of rainforest holds more kinds of living
    thing than anyone could name from memory. Biology needs a filing system —
    and biologists call the making of one <strong>classification</strong>, or
    <strong>taxonomy</strong>. But it cannot be just any filing system. A
    librarian can shelve books by colour. Biology cannot, because its
    collection keeps changing: species evolve, split and die out. What works
    is a system that reflects <strong>actual ancestry</strong>, so that the
    drawer an organism is filed in tells you what it truly <em>is</em>, not
    just what it looks like.
  </p>
  <p>
    Why bother? Four uses. <strong>Identification</strong>: anyone can name an
    unknown organism with the right tool — you will use one in a moment.
    <strong>Prediction</strong>: if a new fungus is filed with the relatives
    of the fungus that gives us penicillin, you can predict what else it
    might produce. <strong>Communication</strong>: farming, medicine and
    conservation all depend on saying exactly which organism was involved.
    <strong>Evolutionary insight</strong>: a classification built on ancestry
    is a map of history.
  </p>
  <p>
    The filing has levels, one inside the other, like folders within folders.
    The basic unit is the <strong>species</strong>: a group of organisms that
    can breed together to produce <em>fertile</em> offspring — young that can
    grow up and have young of their own. Species are grouped into
    <strong>genera</strong> (one of them is a <strong>genus</strong>), then
    into families, orders, classes, phyla, kingdoms and finally
    <strong>domains</strong> — eight levels, each wider than the last.
  </p>
  <p>
    The naming itself is the gift of Carl Linnaeus (1753). Every species gets
    a two-part <strong>binomial</strong> name — "two names". Ours is
    <em>Homo sapiens</em>: genus first, species second, written in italics.
    <em>Panthera leo</em> is the lion. <em>Escherichia coli</em> is the gut
    bacterium. The binomial means the same thing in every language on Earth —
    before Linnaeus, one plant might carry a descriptive name a paragraph
    long, different from author to author.
  </p>
  <p>
    Watch the levels do quiet work on a familiar animal: the domestic cat.
    Kingdom <em>Animalia</em> (eats food, no cell walls). Phylum
    <em>Chordata</em> (has a backbone). Class <em>Mammalia</em> (fur, milk).
    Order <em>Carnivora</em> (shearing teeth). Family <em>Felidae</em>
    (retractile claws). Genus <em>Felis</em>. Species <em>catus</em>. Each
    level up widens the circle of cousins: at family level the cat joins
    lions and leopards, at order level dogs and seals, at class level whales
    and bats. An address like this is a compressed family history — eight
    words that sketch the cat's place in the tree of life.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> living things are filed by ancestry, through
    nested levels, under a two-part name. Now: the tool that lets you file an
    organism yourself.
  </p>

  <h2>Try it yourself: naming something with a key</h2>
  <p>
    A <strong>dichotomous key</strong> — dichotomous means "cut into two" —
    is a forked questionnaire of visible features. At each step you answer one
    either/or question, and the answer sends you to the next fork. Four
    woodland animals, one key:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>1a — has feathers → go to 2. 1b — has fur → go to 3.</li>
    <li>2a — webbed feet → it is a <strong>duck</strong>. 2b — grasping feet → it is an <strong>owl</strong>.</li>
    <li>3a — tail flattened and scaly → it is a <strong>beaver</strong>. 3b — tail bushy → it is a <strong>squirrel</strong>.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Each fork tests one visible character, and after a couple of questions an
    unknown animal lands on a name — no expert needed. Notice that a key does
    not have to reflect ancestry; its job is routing, not history. Keys are
    the everyday, hands-on half of classification.
  </p>

  <h2>The five big groups</h2>
  <p>
    Sort an organism with your eyes and you are usually choosing among five
    great groups. The dividing features are simple: Does the cell have a
    <strong>nucleus</strong> (a control room that holds the DNA)? Is it one
    cell or many? How does it feed?
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Prokaryotes</strong> — bacteria and their relatives the archaea —
    are single cells with <em>no</em> nucleus; their DNA sits in a loop in the
    jelly of the cell. Their walls (in bacteria) are made of a material called
    peptidoglycan, and they reproduce by <strong>binary fission</strong> —
    simply splitting in two — in as little as 20 minutes.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Protoctists</strong> are the odds-and-ends drawer: mostly
    single-celled organisms that <em>do</em> have a nucleus. The shape-shifting
    <em>Amoeba</em>, the green <em>Chlorella</em> and the malaria parasite
    <em>Plasmodium</em> all live here.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Fungi</strong> — yeasts, moulds, mushrooms — have a nucleus but
    cannot photosynthesise. They feed by <em>absorbing</em> dead material
    through a body threaded with microscopic tubes (<strong>hyphae</strong>);
    a mushroom is only the group's fruiting body, like the apple on a tree.
    Their cell walls are made of chitin — the same material as an insect's
    shell.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Plants</strong> are many-celled and make their own sugar by
    photosynthesis, using green chloroplasts, cellulose walls and starch as
    their store. From mosses (no internal pipelines) through ferns to conifers
    and flowering plants, whose flowers, seeds and fruits carried the group
    almost everywhere.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Animals</strong> are many-celled, have no cell walls, and must
    eat: they take food into a body cavity, move with muscles and coordinate
    with nerves. The group splits into <strong>vertebrates</strong> with
    backbones (fish, amphibians, reptiles, birds, mammals — fur and milk for
    the mammals) and the great spineless majority (insects, crustaceans,
    molluscs, segmented worms, jellyfish and their kin).
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "mushrooms are plants." Fungi cannot photosynthesise
  — no chloroplasts, no green pigment — and they feed by absorbing dead material
  through their hyphae, with cell walls of chitin (the material of insect shells, not
  of cellulose). Genetically they sit closer to animals than to plants, which is why
  fungal infections are so hard to treat: anything that kills a fungus can damage its
  animal host, because the biochemistry is kin.</p>

  <h2>From ladder to tree</h2>
  <p>
    One more shift finishes the picture, and it reorganised all of biology.
    Click through the four steps below.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="from ladder to tree — the four facts that reorganised all of life" widget="equationStep" config={treeCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> click through the four steps in order
    and watch the organising picture change — from a ladder ranking life
    "simple" to "complex", to a branching bush with three domains at its
    base. Pause on step 3 and say what a fork means: a common ancestor,
    with closer forks meaning closer relatives. Step 4 is where the modern
    tree actually comes from — DNA sequence similarity, not anatomy
    arguments.
  </p>

  <p>
    The modern tree is drawn as a <strong>cladogram</strong>: a branching
    diagram in which every fork is an inferred <strong>common ancestor</strong>
    and the branch tips are living groups. Three reading rules unlock it.
    First, a closer fork means closer relatives. Second, a genuine
    evolutionary group — a <strong>clade</strong> — must contain an ancestor
    and <em>all</em> of its descendants; "reptiles" excluding birds is a
    leftover of the old ladder, because crocodiles are closer kin to birds
    than to lizards. Third, every living species is equally "evolved": a
    bacterium is not a primitive stop on the way to us, but a cousin with 4
    billion years of its own refinement.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "humans evolved from chimpanzees." No — humans and
  chimpanzees share a common ancestor that lived roughly 7 million years ago, and
  both lineages have been evolving ever since. Chimpanzees are our cousins, not our
  grandparents; a cladogram shows every living species descending from shared nodes,
  never one living species from another.</p>
  <p>
    The scale of the finished tree is humbling. Around 2.1 million species
    have been formally described; the best estimates put the total at 8–10
    million, most of them insects. By biomass and sheer cell count, most of
    the living world is bacterial. And extinction is part of the tree: more
    than 99% of the species that ever lived are gone, their branches ending
    in fossils. The current extinction rate, hundreds of times the natural
    background, is pruning branches faster than we can name them.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "bacteria are germs — the enemy." Only a small
  minority of bacterial species cause disease; the rest run the planet. Soil bacteria
  fix the nitrogen crops need, gut bacteria synthesise vitamins and train the immune
  system, and the oxygen you breathe was invented by cyanobacteria. Sterilising the
  world of bacteria would kill the biosphere long before it killed the pathogens.</p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. The answers are right below.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>A car moves and burns fuel. Why is it not alive?</li>
    <li>Write the two-part name of our species correctly, and say what each part is.</li>
    <li>A many-celled organism has no cell walls and moves with muscles. Which group is it in?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Answers:</strong> (1) It does not do all seven MRS GREN jobs — it
    cannot grow by making new material, reproduce, excrete or respond to the
    world on its own. (2) <em>Homo sapiens</em> — genus first, species second,
    in italics. (3) The animals — no cell walls plus muscle-powered movement
    is the animal signature.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Something is alive only if it does all seven MRS GREN jobs — which is why viruses count as non-living.</li>
    <li>Classification files organisms by actual ancestry, through eight nested levels, under a two-part italic binomial name.</li>
    <li>A dichotomous key routes an unknown organism to a name through either/or questions about visible features.</li>
    <li>The five big groups — prokaryotes, protoctists, fungi, plants, animals — separate by nucleus, cell number and how they feed.</li>
    <li>Fungi are not plants: no photosynthesis, feeding by absorption, walls of chitin, and genetically closer to animals.</li>
    <li>A cladogram's forks are common ancestors; closer forks mean closer relatives, and no living species descends from another living species.</li>
    <li>The tree is now built from molecular evidence, has three domains, and by sheer cell count is mostly bacterial.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    The tree of life is the frame every later biology lesson hangs on.
    Evolution by natural selection explains how its branches formed and are
    still forming, and DNA, genes and heredity supplies the molecule the
    tree is now drawn from. The identification skills here — keys, levels,
    clades — also carry into ecology, where the species being counted have
    to be named first.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    The method behind the modern tree is molecular comparison. DNA and
    protein sequences pick up mutations at roughly steady rates, so the
    number of differences between two species' genes works as a
    <strong>molecular clock</strong>: calibrate it against fossils of known
    age, and it can date branch points where no fossils exist. Cytochrome c,
    a small respiratory protein, differs between human and chimpanzee in zero
    amino acids, between human and horse by about 12, and between human and
    yeast by about 45 — a ladder of kinship measured in a single molecule
    before genome sequencing existed. Today, DNA barcoding (a standard short
    gene region, COI for animals) identifies species from tissue scrapings,
    and environmental DNA from a spoonful of seawater lists the fish that
    swam past — taxonomy now partly happens in a centrifuge.
  </p>
  <p>
    Honest complications keep the field lively. The <strong>species
    problem</strong>: the interbreeding definition fails for asexual bacteria
    (most of life), for ring species (neighbouring populations interbreed but
    the ends do not), and for the 10–20% of plant and animal species that
    hybridise; working biologists fall back on clear, diagnosable
    distinctness, or on "evolutionarily independent lineages".
    <strong>Horizontal gene transfer</strong> — bacteria swapping genes across
    lineages — means the deepest parts of the tree are less a tree than a
    woven thicket, which is why the three-domain root (who branched first,
    bacteria or archaea) remains contested. And the <strong>rank
    problem</strong>: ranks are human conveniences, not natural units —
    "family" in birds and "family" in beetles are not comparable spans of
    time or diversity. The tree is real; the drawers are ours.
  </p>
  <p>
    Finally, the vocabulary the modern tree is written in. A
    <strong>clade</strong> is defined by <strong>shared derived
    characters</strong> (the technical word is synapomorphies) — feathers
    define the bird clade, mammary glands the mammal clade. The method's
    discipline is telling three kinds of similarity apart: <em>derived</em>
    (inherited from a recent common ancestor — informative),
    <em>ancestral</em> (inherited from a much deeper ancestor — uninformative
    about close kin), and <em>analogies</em> (the wings of birds and
    butterflies — similar by function, unrelated by history). Convergent
    evolution is the trap the method exists to disarm: dolphins and sharks
    share a silhouette but not a lineage, and only the derived skeletal and
    molecular characters separate the mammal from the fish. Every branch
    point on the tree of life is, in the end, a claim that some character is
    genuinely shared and derived — which is why the whole edifice now rests
    on DNA, the longest character list we have.
  </p>

